<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
  <!--
    Scanning permissions are declared by the app, not here: app.json and
    plugins/withNigehbanAndroid.js are the one place that decides them, and
    `expo prebuild` regenerates the app manifest from those. This module needs
    BLUETOOTH_SCAN (Android 12+), ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION (below it) and
    ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION for a scan that has to keep matching while the
    app is not in the foreground -- all three are already there.

    The receivers belong here, beside the classes they name.

    BandWakeReceiver is NOT exported. The only thing that reaches it is the
    PendingIntent this module handed to the Bluetooth stack, which the system
    delivers back into our own process; nothing outside the app should be able
    to fake a band emergency.

    BandWakeBootReceiver IS exported, because it has to be: BOOT_COMPLETED and
    the Bluetooth state change are broadcast by the system, and a non-exported
    receiver never sees them. It reads no data from the intent beyond the
    adapter state and acts only on a standing instruction already in our own
    storage, so being reachable buys an attacker the ability to re-register a
    scan the user had already asked for.

    ACTION_STATE_CHANGED and BOOT_COMPLETED are both exempt from the Android 8
    implicit-broadcast ban, so a manifest receiver is still the right shape.
  -->
  <application>
    <receiver
      android:name="com.nigehban.bandwake.BandWakeReceiver"
      android:exported="false" />

    <receiver
      android:name="com.nigehban.bandwake.BandWakeBootReceiver"
      android:exported="true">
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
        <action android:name="android.bluetooth.adapter.action.STATE_CHANGED" />
      </intent-filter>
    </receiver>
  </application>
</manifest>
